<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ali Abdullah Saleh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1947">1947</a>–<a href="/years/2017">2017</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ali Abdullah Saleh was President of North <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> from 1978 and, after unification in 1990, the first President of the Republic of Yemen. He won the civil war of 1994 against southern separatists. Protests in 2011 forced him to hand over power in 2012, and he was killed in 2017 after breaking his alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/houthi-movement">Houthis</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ali-abdullah-saleh.jpg" alt="Ali Abdullah Saleh" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: premier.gov.ru · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ali_Abdullah_Saleh_-_Putin_meeting_2010-06-30_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ali Abdullah Saleh was born on 21 March 1947 to a poor Zaydi family in the village of Beit al-Ahmar, in the territory of the Sanhan clan some 20 kilometres south-east of Sanaa. His father died after divorcing his mother, who then married his late husband's brother Muhammad Saleh, who became Saleh's mentor and stepfather. Saleh joined the Royal Armed Forces of North Yemen as an infantry soldier in 1958 and entered the Royal Military Academy in 1960. He was commissioned a second lieutenant in the armoured corps in 1963, and took part in the military coup of 1962 that removed King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-badr">Muhammad al-Badr</a> and established the Yemen Arab Republic.</p><p>He reached the rank of major during the North Yemen Civil War, trained as a staff officer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> between 1970 and 1971, and became a full colonel in 1976, commanding a mechanised brigade. In 1977 President Ahmad al-Ghashmi appointed him military governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiz">Taiz</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After al-Ghashmi was assassinated on 24 June 1978, Saleh joined a four-man provisional presidency council, and on 17 July 1978 parliament elected him president of the Yemen Arab Republic. On 10 August 1978 he ordered the execution of 30 officers charged with conspiring against him. He survived a serious coup attempt in late 1978, became secretary-general of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/general-peoples-congress">General People's Congress</a> on 30 August 1982 and was re-elected in 1983. Under him Yemen began to produce oil.</p><p>In 1990, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in decline and communist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-yemen">South Yemen</a> weakened, North and South unified. The South accepted Saleh as president, with Ali Salem al-Beidh as vice president. After al-Beidh left the Presidential Council in 1993, a brief civil war ended in July 1994 with the defeat of southern forces. Parliament made Saleh a field marshal in 1997, and he won the presidential election of 1999 with 96.2 per cent of the vote.</p><p>In early 2011, after the Tunisian revolution, protests demanded an end to his three decades in power. On 2 February 2011 he said he would not seek re-election, and on 18 March at least 52 people were killed when government forces fired on demonstrators in Sanaa. He resigned in 2012, and Abdrabbuh Mansur Hadi succeeded him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Saleh allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/houthi-movement">Houthis</a> in 2015, and in July 2016 they formed a Supreme Political Council. In December 2017 he declared that he was leaving the alliance, and on 4 December 2017 he was killed in Sanaa in fighting with Houthi forces. In 2013 he had opened a museum of his 33 years in power in a wing of the Al Saleh Mosque in Sanaa.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ali Abdullah Saleh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Ali Abdullah Saleh</b>, president of the north since 1978, became chairman of a five-member Presidential Council; Ali Salim al-Beidh, secretary-general of the southern Yemeni Socialist Party, became vice-president; and Sanaa became the capital, with Aden designated the economic capital.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-yemen">Republic of Yemen · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In November 1989 the two leaderships agreed a unity constitution, and on 22 May 1990 South Yemen united with the Yemen Arab Republic to form the Republic of Yemen, with <b>Ali Abdullah Saleh</b> as president and al-Beidh as vice-president.</blockquote><a href="/empire/south-yemen">South Yemen · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In Yemen <b>Ali Abdullah Saleh</b> was injured in an assassination attempt in June 2011, signed a Gulf Cooperation Council plan in November and handed power to Abdrabbuh Mansur Hadi in February 2012.</blockquote><a href="/movement/arab-spring">Arab Spring · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>President Ibrahim al-Hamdi was assassinated in 1977, and his successor too, before <b>Ali Abdullah Saleh</b> took power in 1978.</blockquote><a href="/state/yemen-arab-republic">Yemen Arab Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Sanhan, in what is now <a href="/countries/yemen">Yemen</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanaa">Sanaa</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/yemen">Yemen</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=15.27,44.28&amp;year=1992" aria-label="Sanhan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5759 1596 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ali Abdullah Saleh"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6209 1866h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6209 1866h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=15.27,44.28&amp;year=1992">What was here in 1992? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
